    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d364">

                <pb facs="0581" n="479" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg479"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.801/639</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Chief of the Division of
                        Trade Agreements</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Deimel</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-08-16">August 16, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>A revised ship subsidy measure has been introduced into the Senate by Senator
                    Copeland, “by request” (S. 3376, July 29, calendar day, August 6, 1935<anchor
                        n="19" xml:id="d364-f1"/>) in place of the bill which he withdrew following
                    the passage of Congressman Bland’s Bill by the House.<anchor n="20"
                        xml:id="d364-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>This new bill differs in a number of important points not only from Senator
                    Copeland’s earlier bill, but also from the Bland Bill. It provides for subsidies
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) for ship building to meet the difference
                    between foreign and domestic construction costs, and (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >b</hi>) for operation <pb facs="0582" n="480" xml:id="pg480"/> to meet the
                    difference between foreign and domestic operating costs, but contains no
                    provision for the third, “trade penetration” or “fighting ships” subsidy.
                    Likewise, the earlier proposals to give specific authorization to the maritime
                    authority for the fixing of rates in foreign commerce are omitted.</p>
                <p>Accordingly, aside from the inclusion in the declaration of policies that the
                    United States should have: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“… a merchant marine sufficient to carry its domestic water borne
                            commerce and at least one-half of the water borne export and import
                            foreign commerce of the United States,”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p rendition="#flush">The principal features in the earlier subsidy bills to which
                    the Department might have serious objections have been eliminated from this
                    bill.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d364-f1" n="19" xml:id="d364-f1-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Congressional Record</hi>, vol. 79, pt. 12, p.
                    12534.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d364-f2" n="20" xml:id="d364-f2-content">
                <p>The Bland Bill, H. R. 8555, known as the Merchant Marine Act of 1936, was passed
                    by Congress and approved June 29, 1936; 49 Stat. 1985.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
